1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,817
Thousands of years ago, Pythagoras
discovered something extraordinary. When

2
00:00:04,852 --> 00:00:09,265
he plucked a string and then plucked one
exactly half its length, the two notes

3
00:00:09,310 --> 00:00:14,698
sounded beautifully connected — one was
exactly an octave higher. He realized

4
00:00:14,779 --> 00:00:19,713
music wasn't just art — it was
mathematics. Every note you hear has a

5
00:00:19,771 --> 00:00:26,483
specific frequency, measured in vibrations
per second. Middle C vibrates at about 262

6
00:00:26,551 --> 00:00:33,438
times per second. The C above it vibrates
at 524 — exactly double. This mathematical

7
00:00:33,506 --> 00:00:38,128
relationship is why certain combinations
of notes sound harmonious while others

8
00:00:38,198 --> 00:00:43,735
sound clashing. The distance between
notes, called intervals, follows precise

9
00:00:43,817 --> 00:00:47,787
mathematical ratios that our brains are
wired to find pleasing.
